WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Look! Up on the screen! It's a bird! It's a plane!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.579
It's … It's Superman… Lives? Where&nbsp;
did this fight scene come from?

00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:09.319
Warning: spoilers ahead!

00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:13.500
With his long-awaited DC comics blockbuster&nbsp;
"The Flash" finally racing across the finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.060
line into theaters across the country, director&nbsp;
Andy Muschietti has given Superman fans one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.960
the most shocking surprises ever — at least it&nbsp;
would've been shocking if he hadn't spoiled it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.400
weeks ahead of the film's release. Yes, we're&nbsp;
talking about that jaw-dropping, head-scratching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.368
"cameo" appearance from none other than&nbsp;
"Nicolas Cage" in the role of the Man of Steel.

00:00:30.368 --> 00:00:33.660
Much hay was made of his inclusion&nbsp;
in the film after Muschietti leaked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.440
the news. While both the scene and the&nbsp;
uproarious reaction from fans may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.040
been confusing for casual observers,&nbsp;
it's actually a reference to an almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.760
mythological DC comics project that&nbsp;
was shelved many, many years ago.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.380
Having existed only through vague recollections&nbsp;
and test footage, fans familiar with the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.860
have likely been in the clouds themselves&nbsp;
imagining what it might look like when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.520
they finally get a glimpse at the "Superman"&nbsp;
film that might've been. Then again, now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.960
they've actually had a chance to see the scene for&nbsp;
themselves in the context of the uneven multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.088
story of "The Flash," they may now be questioning&nbsp;
if it's worth all the hay in Smallville.

00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:09.420
For those experiencing the legend of Nicolas&nbsp;
Cage's Superman for the first time in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.340
Flash," we'll briefly recap its long, confusing&nbsp;
history in development hell. The story goes that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.080
hot off the success of his indie film "Clerks,"&nbsp;
Kevin Smith was contacted by Warner Bros. to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.400
a new "Superman" film within the universe of Tim&nbsp;
Burton's "Batman" series. Later in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.128
Burton himself would even be attached to direct,&nbsp;
under the prospective title "Superman Lives."&nbsp;

00:01:28.128 --> 00:01:31.740
According to Smith, producer Jon Peters&nbsp;
felt that Superman, as a character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.980
was too queer-coded — which is a nice&nbsp;
way of saying that he kept referring to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.460
the character with homophobic slurs in&nbsp;
his production notes. Because of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.540
bigoted anxiety, Peters gave Smith three&nbsp;
non-negotiable parameters for his story:

00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:45.316
"One: I don't want to see him in that suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.244
Two: I don't want to see him fly. And three: He's&nbsp;
got to fight a giant spider in the third act."

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:56.400
Unfortunately, these were far from the last&nbsp;
bizarre notes Smith or Burton would hear during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.120
production, most of which concerned the addition&nbsp;
of scenes and characters that were just there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.800
to sell merchandise. Either because of genuine&nbsp;
quality concerns, the rapidly inflating budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.558
or a lack of confidence following the abysmal&nbsp;
response to 1997's "Batman &amp; Robin," "Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.558 --> 00:02:15.600
Lives" was shelved for good in 1998, doomed to&nbsp;
tease fans for 25 years. But now that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.307
some part of this promise finally fulfilled,&nbsp;
was it really worth all the hype? Well …

00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:20.967
"NO!"

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:24.780
The problem with the lightning-fast "Superman&nbsp;
Lives" sequence is the same problem that follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.100
the rest of the forced cameos in "The Flash." The&nbsp;
scene plays out with Cage's Superman fighting off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.660
a giant spider, and once he defeats the spider&nbsp;
he just looks directly at the screen. The idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.500
seems to be that these cameo world's will be&nbsp;
destroyed if the Flash doesn't undo his time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.220
travel actions. But its introduced so last&nbsp;
minute that it never had as much weight as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.900
it should have. Even ignoring the fact that the&nbsp;
CGI model for Cage is far from perfect, the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.208
itself is merely slapped onto the outside of&nbsp;
the story with no set-up, payoff, or impact.

00:02:50.208 --> 00:02:53.640
If you were looking for a meaningful glimpse&nbsp;
at Tim Burton and Kevin Smith's scrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.660
"Superman" project, you're likely gonna be&nbsp;
disappointed. However, for those of us that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.580
go to these multiverse films to feast on as many&nbsp;
Easter eggs as we possibly can, Nicolas Cage's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.300
uncanny turn as Superman makes "The Flash"&nbsp;
every bit the masterpiece it was said to be.

